\textbf{4. Total Variation Distance (TVD).} Using $f(u) = \frac{1}{2}|u-1|$, TVD measures the maximum absolute probability difference over any event, $\sup_{A} |P(A) - Q(A)|$. TVD provides a robust distance metric irrespective of extreme log-probability ratios, though its non-differentiability at $u=1$ challenges direct optimization in neural spaces. The choice from this f-divergence family dictates how the student interpolates teacher knowledge during on-policy generation.

\subsection{A Unified Mathematical View of Modern OPD}
\label{subsec:unified_view}

Recent OPD methods can be unified under a single objective by decoupling the sampling distribution (which dictates the trajectory) from the divergence metric (which dictates local token-level matching). The generalized OPD objective is
\begin{equation}
    \loss_{\mathrm{OPD}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{y \sim \pi_{\mathrm{mix}}} \left[ \sum_{t=1}^{|y|} \mathcal{D}_f \left( \pteacher(\cdot | x, y_{<t}) \parallel \ptheta(\cdot | x, y_{<t}) \right) \right]
\end{equation}
where $\pi_{\mathrm{mix}}$ is the behavioral policy driving state exploration and $\mathcal{D}_f$ is the chosen f-divergence. The key modern methods---GKD \citep{2306.13649}, MiniLLM \citep{2306.08543}, and DistiLLM \citep{2402.03898}---all map onto this equation as distinct parameterizations of a continuous theoretical space.

\textbf{Generalized Knowledge Distillation (GKD) \citep{2306.13649}.} GKD addresses trajectory mismatch by defining $\pi_{\mathrm{mix}}$ as an explicit interpolation between the dataset and the student's on-policy distribution. The output sequence is drawn with probability $\lambda$ from $\ptheta$ and with probability $1-\lambda$ from $\mathcal{D}$. GKD is flexible regarding $\mathcal{D}_f$, empirically testing Forward KL, Reverse KL, and JSD. Setting $\lambda \to 1$ makes GKD purely on-policy.

\textbf{MiniLLM \citep{2306.08543}.} MiniLLM identifies that Forward KL forces over-allocation to the teacher's long tail. To enforce mode-seeking behavior, it selects Reverse KL ($\mathcal{D}_f = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\ptheta \parallel \pteacher)$). For sampling, MiniLLM employs a teacher-mixed strategy: with probability $\alpha = 0.2$, tokens are drawn from the teacher to alleviate reward hacking, making $\pi_{\mathrm{mix}} = (1 - \alpha)\ptheta + \alpha \pteacher$. Because Reverse KL places the student parameters in both the expectation and the log-ratio, MiniLLM reformulates optimization via the Policy Gradient theorem (REINFORCE), treating the teacher's log-probability as a reward
\begin{equation}
    \nabla_\theta \loss_{\mathrm{MiniLLM}} = \mathbb{E}_{y \sim \ptheta} \left[ \sum_{t=1}^{|y|} \left( \log \frac{\ptheta(y_t|y_{<t})}{\pteacher(y_t|y_{<t})} - 1 \right) \nabla_\theta \log \ptheta(y_t|y_{<t}) \right]
\end{equation}
To mitigate the high variance of policy gradients, MiniLLM introduces reward baselines from the single-step Reverse KL decomposition, bridging RL optimization with knowledge distillation.

\textbf{DistiLLM \citep{2402.03898}.} While MiniLLM theoretically achieves mode-seeking behavior, reliance on high-variance RL causes empirical instability. DistiLLM resolves this with a Skewed Reverse KL divergence: it defines a skewed mixture $\tilde{P} = \alpha \pteacher + (1-\alpha) \ptheta$ and minimizes $\KL(\ptheta \parallel \tilde{P})$, which inherits the mode-seeking geometry of Reverse KL while avoiding the zero-division instability when $\ptheta(y) > 0$ but $\pteacher(y) \approx 0$, because the mixture $\tilde{P}$ is always non-zero wherever $\ptheta$ is. This allows standard cross-entropy optimization without policy gradients. Under our unified view, DistiLLM maintains $\pi_{\mathrm{mix}} = \ptheta$ but reparameterizes $\mathcal{D}_f$ for tractability and stability.

This unified perspective demonstrates that modern OPD is not a series of disparate heuristics, but a systematic, progressive tightening of bounds governing trajectory sampling and geometric distribution matching.


\section{Taxonomy of On-Policy Distillation}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

To navigate the expanding OPD literature, we propose a multi-dimensional taxonomy. Rather than categorizing methods by chronological release or minor architectural quirks, we classify them along three fundamental dimensions: (1) \textbf{Feedback Signal}, dictating what information flows from teacher to student; (2) \textbf{Teacher Access}, reflecting deployment constraints on the teacher model; and (3) \textbf{Granularity}, describing the temporal resolution of the distillation loss.

